00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.675
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.675 --> 00:00:05.430
I help some security experts
with their function times.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.140
Will we crack the code?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.160
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.550
Mega Ultra VPN is a
personal security group

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.865
working to help companies manage
their cryptokeys in the cloud.

00:00:17.865 --> 00:00:19.740
Recently, part of their
system was moved over

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.180
to Google Cloud
Functions in order

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.280
to reduce operational
complexity.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.290
They reached out after
their initial port

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.182
as they were seeing less than
ideal response times for Google

00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:29.640
Cloud Functions,
and not to mention

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.497
a high number of instances.

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:34.080
To figure out what was going on,
we deconstructed the codebase

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.450
and ran a few
different V8 profiles

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.750
to figure out where the most
CPU intensive areas were.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.610
To no one's surprise,
our security company

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.010
was spending the
majority of their API

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.900
calls sitting in
crypto functions

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.940
that were computing hash
keys for various data

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:48.990
that was coming in.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.360
Now, traditionally, I'd
want to dig into the code

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.120
and see where we can
optimize the CPU cycles,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.310
but when you're dealing
with crypto algorithms,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.510
I've learned to
just leave them be.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.090
So instead, it made
a lot more sense

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.490
for our team to simply
upgrade their GCF

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.990
instances to use more RAM.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.150
Now, as listed in
the documentation,

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.120
RAM provisions are connected to
higher performing CPUs, which,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.060
based our tests, could more
effectively crunch the crypto

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.490
numbers and, hopefully,
bring down costs.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.110
To figure out if
this would help us,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.690
we set up a simple test by
deploying a sample crypto

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.290
code set and flooded it with
requests over five minutes.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.350
For our 512meg
instances, we capped out

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.510
at about 25
invocations a second,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.630
taking around 60k milliseconds
per each response.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.370
However, when we
moved to 2 gigabytes,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.680
we ended up with 35
invocations a second,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.170
and our response time capped
out at around 15k milliseconds.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.140
Now after seeing our
tests, our crypto friends

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.180
threw more CPU power
at their problem

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.761
and brought their response
times down significantly.

00:01:44.761 --> 00:01:46.260
To learn more about
some of the code

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.345
we saw today, check
out the article linked

00:01:48.345 --> 00:01:49.780
in the description below.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.240
And don't forget to check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.040
Performance Atlas
content set because, when

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.620
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

